window.docContents[218].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra/#Chapter2___Example2___3___3":{"contents":"Example 2.3.3.\n\n1. V = 0.2. V = A, and \\rho : A \\to \\operatorname{End} A is defined as follows: \\rho(a) is the operator of left multiplication by a, so that \\rho(a)b = ab (the usual product). This representation is called the regular representation of A. Similarly, one can equip A with a structure of a right A-module by setting \\rho(a)b := ba.3. A = k. Then a representation of A is simply a vector space over k.4. A = k\\langle x_1, \\ldots, x_n \\rangle. Then a representation of A is just a vector space V over k with a collection of arbitrary linear operators \\rho(x_1), \\ldots, \\rho(x_n) : V \\to V (explain why!).\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Examples of representations: zero, regular, k, free algebra","header":"Examples of representations: zero, regular, k, free algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Examples-of-representations___-zero___-regular___-k___-free-algebra/#Chapter2___Example2___3___3"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Section-2___9___-Lie-algebras-___-heading-and-skew-symmetric-bilinear-map/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___9_heading":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Section 2.9: Lie algebras — heading and skew-symmetric bilinear map","header":"Section 2.9: Lie algebras — heading and skew-symmetric bilinear map","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Section-2___9___-Lie-algebras-___-heading-and-skew-symmetric-bilinear-map/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___9_heading"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Example2___9___12":{"contents":"Example 2.9.12. The associative algebra U(\\mathfrak{sl}(2)) is the algebra generated by e, f, h, with relations\n\nhe - eh = 2e, \\qquad hf - fh = -2f, \\qquad ef - fe = h.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.9. Lie algebras\u0009Universal enveloping algebra of sl(2)","header":"Universal enveloping algebra of sl(2)","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___9___-Lie-algebras/Universal-enveloping-algebra-of-sl_LPAR_2_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Example2___9___12"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Every finite-dimensional module over a nonempty full matrix algebra is linearly equivalent to a finite product of copies of its standard module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived13; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived14; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived15; role=supporting]\n\nThe standard column module over a nonempty full matrix algebra is simple.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived12; role=supporting]\n\nEvery finite-dimensional simple module over a nonempty full matrix algebra is linearly equivalent to the standard column module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=primary]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3/Derived15; role=supporting]\n\nComponent idempotents multiply to the selected idempotent at equal indices and to zero at distinct indices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=primary]\n\nThe sum of the component idempotents in a finite product of rings is one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.3.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\u0009Alternative proof of Theorem 3.3.1 without earlier results\u0009Alternative proof of Theorem 3.3.1 without earlier results\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Alternative-proof-of-Theorem-3___3___1-without-earlier-results--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation--Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula--Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"An auxiliary statement whose displayed formal type is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.9.2; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary result whose formal statement is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Discussion_proof_of_Theorem5.9.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Remark5.9.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.9. The Frobenius formula for the character of an induced representation\u0009Alternative form of the Frobenius formula\u0009Alternative form of the Frobenius formula\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation/Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___9___-The-Frobenius-formula-for-the-character-of-an-induced-representation--Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula--Alternative-form-of-the-Frobenius-formula--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Summary___-finitely-many-indecomposables___-last-part-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Discussion_after_Corollary6___8___3":{"contents":"These two corollaries show that there are only finitely many indecomposable representations (since there are only finitely many roots) and that the dimension vector of each of them is a positive root. The last statement of Gabriel's theorem follows from\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.8. Proof of Gabriel's theorem\u0009Summary: finitely many indecomposables, last part of Gabriel's theorem","header":"Summary: finitely many indecomposables, last part of Gabriel's theorem","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___8___-Proof-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/Summary___-finitely-many-indecomposables___-last-part-of-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Chapter6___Discussion_after_Corollary6___8___3"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Morphisms-of-complexes___-cocycles___-coboundaries___-bounded-complexes/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___8___1":{"contents":"There is an obvious notion of a morphism of complexes. Namely, a morphism f : C \\to D is a collection of morphisms f_i : C_i \\to D_i such that d_i \\circ f_i = f_{i+1} \\circ d_i. Clearly, such morphisms can be composed, which makes the class of all complexes over \\mathcal{C} into a category, denoted \\operatorname{Compl}(\\mathcal{C}).\n\nIn particular, one can consider complexes of abelian groups, vector spaces, modules over a ring, etc. In this case, elements of \\operatorname{Ker}(d_i) are called i-cocycles, the elements of \\operatorname{Im}(d_{i-1}) are called i-coboundaries, and the elements of H^i(C) are called ith cohomology classes. This terminology is adopted from topology. For this reason, exact sequences are also called acyclic complexes (as they are complexes which have no nontrivial cocycles, i.e., cocycles that are not coboundaries).\n\nOften one considers complexes that are bounded in one or both directions; i.e., the objects C_i are zero for i \\gg 0, i \\ll 0, or both. In this case one writes one zero on each side where the complex is bounded. For example, a complex bounded on both sides with n + 1 terms will look like\n\n0 \\to C_0 \\to C_1 \\to \\cdots \\to C_n \\to 0.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology\u0009Morphisms of complexes, cocycles, coboundaries, bounded complexes","header":"Morphisms of complexes, cocycles, coboundaries, bounded complexes","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Morphisms-of-complexes___-cocycles___-coboundaries___-bounded-complexes/#Chapter7___Discussion_after_Definition7___8___1"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category--Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"When a finite group's cardinality is invertible in the field, the module category over its monoid algebra satisfies the displayed auxiliary category property.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Example7.9.5; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.9. Exact functors\u0009Representations of finite groups as semisimple category\u0009Representations of finite groups as semisimple category\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___9___-Exact-functors/Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___9___-Exact-functors--Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category--Representations-of-finite-groups-as-semisimple-category--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Guarantees a finite algebraic candidate meeting the three displayed conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=primary]\n\nProduces a finite algebraic candidate satisfying the displayed conditions, with a rank bound and uniqueness up to algebra equivalence among the specified candidates.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=primary]\n\nGuarantees a finite algebraic candidate meeting the two displayed conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=primary]\n\nBounds the rank of a candidate by that of the ambient algebra under the stated conditions.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter9/Corollary9.7.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.7. Morita equivalence\u0009Uniqueness of basic algebra for finite abelian categories\u0009Uniqueness of basic algebra for finite abelian categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___7___-Morita-equivalence/Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras--9___7___-Morita-equivalence--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Uniqueness-of-basic-algebra-for-finite-abelian-categories--Formalization--Primary-declarations"}});